    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nNvim gained `:bcd` in #33318. Unlike `:tcd` / `:lcd`, it (intentionally) does not have \"sticky\" behavior. \n\nArguments for/against sticky `:bcd`:\n\npro:\n\n1. Using a file-picker, `:find`, etc. from e.g. a `:terminal` buffer with a buf-dir, typically the file-picker uses CWD. So when a file is selected, the user expects the opened file to also have that buf-dir.\n    - Workaround: the file-picker can do `:bcd` on file-open. (opt-in \"inherit\")\n    - Workaround: user can opt-in to `:help project-dir` (vim-rooter approach).\n\ncon:\n\n1. `:bcd` becomes more of a \"commitment\" that you have to \"manage\".\n2. Opening a file from project B while in project A gives it A's dir.\n3. Because win-dir is inheritable, it's a \"weak\" signal of intent, whereas buf-dir indicates the user (or script) explicitly called `:bcd`. That is a useful signal.\n4. vim-rooter use-case is a bit more awkward (you need to opt-in and also opt-out via `:bcd!`). \n5. files opened from dir.lua (directory browser) inherit `:bcd`, which will confuse users that otherwise aren't aware of, or don't care about, `:bcd`\n    - this also hints at an interesting general property: non-sticky `:bcd` is useful for plugins that want to temporarily set CWD without \"leaking\" into the rest of the user's actions.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nMake a decision...\n\nCurrently it feels like non-sticky is the right approach because it's simpler to understand, and users can easily opt-in to more aggressive vim-rooter style behavior (`:help project-dir`).\n\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "To clarify, \"sticky\" `:bcd` behavior means that when I open a new buffer **B** from buffer **A**,  **B** \"inherits\" the same buffer-local directory as **A**.\n\n`:lcd` is sticky. If I do `:lcd /some/dir` and then `:sp`, the new window will also have the same window-local directory `/some/dir`.\n\nBy contrast, `:bcd` is intentionally NOT sticky right now. \n\n\n```\n:cd $HOME\n:e mybuf\n:bcd /some/dir\n:term pwd \" shows $HOME (not /some/dir)\n```\n\nEDIT: term's behavior was fixed in #41211. This example is no longer relevant. Here's another example of non-sticky behavior:\n\n```\n$ ls bcd-example\nhello.c  main.c  tags\n$ nvim bcd-example/main.c\n\n:bcd bcd-example\n:tj say_hello \" jumps to hello.c\n```\n\n`:tj say_hello` will open `hello.c` in the global CWD",
