        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "If all of the existing structures are currently \"sticky\", then I think we should follow through with the mental model and make buffers sticky as well. Like @echasnovski said, this sounds like a source of confusion and subtle bugs. Not only from a user's standpoint, but also from a developer's - you need to actively remember that buffers do not carry over their directories and that *only for buffers* do you need to set up an autocommand or other mechanism to maintain a changed buffer-local directory.\n\nI'm not sure of the exact internals of `gd` and other LSP keybinds, but wouldn't sticky buffers make it easier to track where you are as you move between definitions and code? When doing `gd` from a buffer the newly opened buffers would have a consistent CWD.\n\nHot take: I personally am a proponent of disabling \"stickiness\" for everything - tabpages, windows and buffers, and making it all opt-in. That objectively gives the simplest behaviour at the cost of a fairly large breakage for existing workflows. My logic is that if someone wants \"stickiness\" across windows, they can set a tabpage-local working directory. If they want stickiness across buffers, they can enable a window-local working directory. Each element subsumes the next. This does give an edge case for one buffer in multiple windows, but it at least gives highly consistent and easy to understand behaviour. Autocommands can be used to automatically emulate \"stickiness\" anyway.\nHowever, I'm not assuming such a drastic change would be considered, which is why I think we should just keep sticky :p",
