        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> \"opening\" a buffer does not always imply a new buffer. Whereas \"opening\" a tab/win always implies a new tab/win\n\nAn argument against sticky `:bcd` is that it's not clear what should happen if we \"open\" a buffer and it already exists. Consider this example:\n\n```\n:bcd /foo\n:e /bar/file2\n```\n\nBuffer `/bar/file2` either exists or is created.\n\n- If it's created (and `:bcd` is sticky) then it must inherit `/foo` as its buffer-local directory\n- If it exists (and `:bcd` is sticky) then should it set the buffer-local directory to `/foo` or leave it as it is?\n    - If we set it, then we might override an exiting buffer-local directory in `/bar/file2`. Some might find that annoying.\n    - If we leave it as it is, then `:bcd` becomes half-sticky. Sticky when it creates new buffers. Non-sticky when it doesn't. How do you feel about this inconsistency?\n\n> I'm not sure of the exact internals of gd and other LSP keybinds, but wouldn't sticky buffers make it easier to track where you are as you move between definitions and code?\n\n`gd`, `:tj`, and `:e` are all affected by this because they sometimes open buffers that already exist. Any \"get or create buffer\" behavior (in plugins or core) will also be affected.\n\nBy contrast, non-sticky `:bcd` behaves more consistently. It acts like an option; a buffer just declares the directory it cares about. Sticky behavior must be achieved explicitly\n\n<details>\n<summary>sticky bcd autocmd</summary>\n\nOne way to opt-in to sticky behavior for new buffers:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"BufNew\", {\n  callback = function(ev)\n    local global_cwd = vim.fn.getcwd(-1, -1, -1)\n    local buffer_cwd = vim.fn.getcwd(-1, -1, 0)\n\n    if global_cwd ~= buffer_cwd then\n      vim.api.nvim_buf_call(ev.buf, function()\n        vim.fn.chdir(buffer_cwd, \"buffer\")\n      end)\n    end\n  end\n})\n```\n\n</details>",
            "created_at": "2026-08-08T17:17:05Z",
