        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Buffer `/bar/file2` either exists or is created.\n> \n> * If it exists (and `:bcd` is sticky) then should it set the buffer-local directory to `/foo` or leave it as it is?\n>     * If we leave it as it is, then `:bcd` becomes half-sticky. Sticky when it creates new buffers. Non-sticky when it doesn't. How do you feel about this inconsistency?\n\nIf `:edit` resulted in showing already existing buffer, then there shouldn't be any changes to its buffer-local directory. If it was set - it is unchanged; if it was not set - it is not set. This exactly how window- and tabpage-local working directories work in relation to creating or navigating to windows and tabpages.\n\n---\n\n> so far, no one has addressed the \"cons\" I listed for sticky `:bcd`, and the only argument in favor has been \"consistency\" (which as I mentioned, is not fully achievable).\n\n> 1. `:bcd` becomes more of a \"commitment\" that you have to \"manage\".\n\nI don't think it is a con. Setting a more narrowly scoped working directory *is* indicating a commitment that can't / shouldn't be managed.\n\n>     2. Opening a file from project B while in project A gives it A's dir.\n\nOnly if buffer-local directory is set. If it is set, it is an indicator there was a strong reason for this behavior. And vim-rooter / project-dir automation helps with this.\n\n>     3. Because win-dir is inheritable, it's a \"weak\" signal of intent, whereas buf-dir indicates the user (or script) explicitly called `:bcd`. That is a useful signal.\n\nI am not sure what use this signal can be good for. Setting buffer-local directory is a strong signal in itself.\n\n>     4. vim-rooter use-case is a bit more awkward (you need to opt-in and also opt-out via `:bcd!`).\n\nExactly the same how it currently needs to be for window- and tabpage-local directories.\n\n>     6. files opened from dir.lua (directory browser) inherit `:bcd`, which will confuse users that otherwise aren't aware of, or don't care about, `:bcd`>        \n>        * this also hints at an interesting general property: non-sticky `:bcd` is useful for plugins that want to temporarily set CWD without \"leaking\" into the rest of the user's actions.\n\nIt is a matter of adjusting the `dir.lua` itself to accommodate the issue. Explicitly unsetting the buffer-local directory for the opened buffer doesn't seem to be a problem. And it is something that everything using `:bcd` needs to take care of since it adds a strong signal (stronger than already present `:lcd`).",
            "created_at": "2026-08-08T18:24:01Z",
